# Important Terms

- Architecture — high-level organization of a system.
- Microarchitecture — internal implementation of an architectural block.
- TLM — Transaction-Level Model.
- Functional Model — high-level model representing expected system behavior.
- Golden Reference — trusted reference used to generate expected results.
- DUT — Design Under Test.
- Scoreboard — compares actual and expected results.
- PPA — Power, Performance, Area.
- RTL — Register Transfer Level.
- HDL — Hardware Description Language.
- Clock-to-Q delay — delay from clock edge to flip-flop output.
- Propagation delay — delay through combinational logic.
- Setup time — required data stability before clock edge.
- Hold time — required data stability after clock edge.
- Synthesizable RTL — RTL that can be converted into hardware by synthesis.

---

# Key Takeaways

- Architecture defines the high-level organization of the chip.
- Microarchitecture defines the internal implementation of an architectural block.
- A functional/TLM model represents expected system behavior.
- The functional model can act as a golden reference.
- The DUT is the design being verified.
- A scoreboard compares actual DUT results with expected results.
- PPA means Power, Performance and Area.
- RTL represents hardware using registers and combinational logic.
- Clock-to-Q, propagation delay and setup time contribute to the simplified minimum clock period.
- Setup concerns data arriving too late.
- Hold concerns data arriving too early.
- HDL coding style influences the hardware inferred by synthesis.
- Not every HDL construct is synthesizable.